    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1405">

                <pb facs="1134" n="1124" xml:id="pg1124"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00B/6–649: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Tsingtao</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hawthorne</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tsingtao</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-06-06">June 6, 1949.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 5—11:53 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>243. President Soviet Citizens Society, accompanied by Danish Consul, Saturday
                    attempted call CC Military Headquarters apply vehicle passes, refused admission,
                    although reportedly a liaison officer to deal with such matters has been
                    appointed.</p>
                <p>Although orders to sentries still somewhat confused, now possible foreigners
                    including American staff enter, leave Consulate General and believe
                    communications can be maintained.</p>
                <p>Exchange rate announced today US $1 equals 5.50 PN;<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d1405-f1"/> Yuan Shihkai $1 ditto; gold bars PN 45,000 per
                    ounce.</p>
                <p>Sent Nanking 177; repeated Department 243, Shanghai 95.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hawthorne</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
